ality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"." 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328655 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328656 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328657 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328658 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328659 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1041 "selection" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328658 13194139535053 true] [17592186328654 1110 #uuid "6973fbac-7f17-5636-9b38-675bc12fb5aa" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328655 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328655 1111 #uuid "4de6557c-c036-5824-abad-778d8b9bc0bc" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328656 1034 "III and IV only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328656 1111 #uuid "4735019f-71c1-5222-a514-bf72cc2b79f3" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328657 1034 "I, II, and IV only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328657 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328657 1111 #uuid "df758e5f-a3e4-57af-9ef9-983306092c5f" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328658 1034 "II, III, and IV only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328658 1111 #uuid "49bf5f50-7541-5002-b37d-f5ef1933d843" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328659 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328659 1111 #uuid "aa7383ea-77b0-5f8a-8079-c7a6fe2410b9" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328660 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328661 13194139535053 true] [17592186328660 1089 "q-333546" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328660 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535053 true] [17592186328660 1100 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =6,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-6Δx}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.10.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328660 1113 #uuid "a490b5bf-1c3d-5695-b7bd-9b32b44f2c4b" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328660 1145 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-6y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-6{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+6{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+6Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}.\n$$\n\nThis shows that the statement I is false.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+6Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.10.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328662 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328663 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328664 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328665 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328666 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1041 "selection" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328663 13194139535053 true] [17592186328661 1110 #uuid "077621c3-8b4e-5429-a3b7-de56b75f374e" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328662 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328662 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328662 1111 #uuid "c49a982c-00f1-5f56-b410-632241896d85" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328663 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328663 1111 #uuid "17ca09d9-29b3-5e5a-94ad-fd371c587e38" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328664 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328664 1111 #uuid 