 is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-5Δx|}{|2+5Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-5Δx| & <|2+5Δx| \\\\ (2-5Δx)^{2} & <(2+5Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} & <4+20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -20Δx & <20Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=1,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." 13194139535053 true] [17592186328668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328669 13194139535053 true] [17592186328668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328670 13194139535053 true] [17592186328668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328671 13194139535053 true] [17592186328668 1000 #edb/ref 17592