n of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}-4({y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-8{y}_{n}+16{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+8{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}<Δx-\\frac{1}{4} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it does not satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535053 true]