[17592186328709 1145 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.4.$ Since this value falls within the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <\\lambda Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <0.4<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method must be stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328711 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328712 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328713 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328714 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328715 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1041 "selection" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328712 13194139535053 true] [17592186328710 1110 #uuid "bb505fcb-3460-5c64-8717-52754d409276" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328711 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328711 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328711 1111 #uuid "ff62623f-80b6-5e6c-b59c-9ae50954d52e" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328712 1034 "I, III, and IV only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328712 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328712 1111 #uuid "81cf23e6-538f-5650-b225-35ae03c3492f" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328713 1034 "I only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328713 1111 #uuid "071050d8-f70c-5390-aa66-ff6559267bd0" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328714 1034 "III and IV only" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328714 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328714 1111 #uuid "32c32dfe-75ef-5910-bc36-8765fced4c21" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328715 1034 "I, II, III, and IV" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535053 true] [17592186328715 1111 #uuid "877a6cc3-a0d0-5c13-9d0a-a88c2d946e05" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328716 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328717 13194139535053 true] [17592186328716 1089 "q-334255" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328716 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535053 true] [17592186328716 1100 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.95$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328716 1113 #uuid "59e45de1-7b70-5e60-9513-37075857ee83" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328716 1145 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.95,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.95)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.95}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.95}| \\\\  & =0.5128…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+2.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3.3}| \\\\  & =0.3030…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139535053 true] [17592186328717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328718 1319413953505